    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, Hope is not a strategy, but I am always hopeful. If I weren't an opt-in list, I wouldn't have served in Congress for nine terms while I got more and more partisan. But just saying, Joe Biden, our president, was the chair for decades of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. And he supports Congress doing this. That's different. And the chairman of the committee, Bob Menendez of New Jersey, supports doing this, and the House has made noises to do it. And so I think there is a possibility. I also think with this push, which may never get anywhere toward bipartisanship, that some Republicans are stepping up and they are to support some Democrats. Problem is that the business model of Congress is basically broken. It's blame the other side for not solving the problem because if you work with the other side, you are bipartisan and that makes you dogmate in your primary and getting reelected is now more important than solving problems for the country. And that is a tragedy. And that is weakening Joe Biden's effectiveness abroad. I mean, I think he's pretty effective. And I think his team is the A team. And he's our first foreign, experienced foreign policy president since George Bush 41. So over four presidencies, one I make in the book. But to have America in this much turmoil makes him weaker.",
