    "turn_text": "coastal district and everybody shops for for homegrown, you know, yummy veggies on the weekends. And it was a good way to meet folks. So I would sit on my car ticket and everybody would rant about how can the foreign aid budget eat up 50% of our annual budget and why as in point, the real answer is 0.001%. But people had no idea what our foreign policy foreign age posture was. And this has gone on for years. Nobody knows nothing. So the Biden approach is to my shorthand. And Jake Sullivan, who I talked to once about this, agrees, is to take the foreign out of foreign policy and to try to make what we do abroad relevant to what we do at home. And the articulation a few months ago by Secretary of State Tony Blinken and National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan was that the foreign policy of Biden is restore alliances, repair alliances, promote democracy, make the world safer, and do three other things which are important and then they're related. They also have to do with the world. One is solve the pandemic for the world, not just for the US. And we made some strides at the G7 last week, maybe not enough, but strides. And another one is climate, which is a huge part of our security at home and security in the world. Think about it. Half the refugees in the world are climate refugees. They're not terrorism refugees. The third part is terrorism. It's still a problem abroad, but it's also a problem domestically. And none of these things was articulated before as a strategy. And average folks who come to talk to their members of Congress around the card table can get that. And I think that that's exactly what Biden is doing and working on. And his trip last week, I thought, was quite successful and focused on all this.",
